---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import LessonIllustration from "../../components/LessonIllustration.astro";
import RegisterStructureDemo from "../../components/RegisterStructureDemo.astro";
import StructureFacts from "../../components/StructureFacts.astro";
import TermCallout from "../../components/TermCallout.astro";
import "../../styles/set-lesson.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="MvRegister"
  description="Alice and Bob keep concurrent trail-status reports until Carol can inspect and resolve them."
  territory="Structures"
  sheetTitle="MvRegister"
>
  <article class="register-lesson">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="territory-label">Structures</p>
      <h1>MvRegister</h1>
      <p class="structure-summary">
        The ranger needs one trail status for the morning bulletin. Alice
        reports that Eagle Creek is open while Bob reports that it is closed.
        Neither hiker has seen the other's note, so the ranger cannot treat
        either report as a correction.
      </p>
      <LessonIllustration id="structures-multi-value-register" />
    </header>

    <StructureFacts
      family="CRDT"
      model="State-based multi-value register"
      updates="Causally tagged string writes"
      merge="Keep writes that are concurrent; replace only observed history."
      delivery="Safe under repeated and out-of-order delivery"
      bestFor="One field where valid concurrent answers need explicit resolution"
      cost="Surviving values, write identities, and causal context"
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="siblings-title">
      <h2 id="siblings-title">Two notebooks hold two current answers</h2>
      <p>
        Alice writes <code>Trail open</code> in her notebook and marks it as
        her first write, <code>A:1</code>. Before that note reaches Bob, he
        writes <code>Trail closed</code> and marks it <code>B:1</code>. Each
        notebook contains one valid local answer, but neither write includes
        evidence that its writer saw the other report.
      </p>
      <p>
        The writes are concurrent. A <dfn><a href="/glossary/#multi-value-register">multi-value register</a></dfn>
        {" "}keeps both as alternatives. Each surviving alternative is a
        {" "}<dfn><a href="/glossary/#sibling">sibling</a></dfn>.
      </p>
      <TermCallout
        term="multi-value register"
        definition="A replicated register that retains concurrent writes until a write that observes them supersedes them."
      />
      <TermCallout
        term="sibling"
        definition="A surviving version of a register value with its own write identity and causal history."
        inlineOnly
      />
      <figure>
        <table aria-label="MvRegister notebook checkpoints">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Notebook</th>
              <th scope="col">Before notes are exchanged</th>
              <th scope="col">After both notes arrive</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Alice</th>
              <td>Trail open <code>[A:1]</code></td>
              <td>Trail open <code>[A:1]</code><br />Trail closed <code>[B:1]</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Bob</th>
              <td>Trail closed <code>[B:1]</code></td>
              <td>Trail open <code>[A:1]</code><br />Trail closed <code>[B:1]</code></td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Carol</th>
              <td>No report</td>
              <td>Trail open <code>[A:1]</code><br />Trail closed <code>[B:1]</code></td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <figcaption>The write identities show that neither report followed the other.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <p>
        The order in which Carol receives the notes does not settle the
        disagreement. After every note arrives, all three notebooks contain
        the same two alternatives.
      </p>
    </section>

    <RegisterStructureDemo
      kind="mv-register"
      title="Keep both trail reports"
      description="Run Alice and Bob's preset race, or choose a trail status for any hiker. The notebooks retain writes that did not observe each other."
      rule="Keep concurrent writes as siblings."
      raceLabel="Race Alice's and Bob's reports"
    />

    <section aria-labelledby="resolve-title">
      <h2 id="resolve-title">Carol resolves what she observed</h2>
      <p>
        Carol's notebook now holds both siblings and the combined history
        <code>[A:1, B:1]</code>. She walks to Eagle Creek, inspects the bridge,
        and chooses a new trail status. Her new note records that she wrote it
        after seeing both earlier reports.
      </p>
      <p>
        When Alice and Bob receive Carol's note, it replaces the two siblings
        that Carol observed. It cannot replace a report that Carol had not yet
        received. That unseen report would remain as another current
        alternative.
      </p>
      <p>
        The deeper Atlas lab exposes the write tags, causal context, delayed
        messages, and observed resolution step.
      </p>
      <p class="lesson-entry">
        <a href="/atlas/multi-value-registers/">Open the causal MvRegister lab</a>
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="source-title">
      <h2 id="source-title">Sources and implementation</h2>
      <p>
        The demo runs Watershed's{" "}
        <a href="https://github.com/tylerbutler/watershed/blob/4a8739323ee491f353fcaa8ccfb0488419c1cd43/src/watershed/mv_register_kernel.gleam">MV-register kernel</a>
        {" "}through the Atlas toolkit.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="structure-next" aria-label="Continue through register structures">
      <a href="/structures/lww-register/">Back to LWWRegister</a>
      <a href="/structures/registers/">Browse register family</a>
      <a href="/structures/register-collection/">Continue to RegisterCollection</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lesson-entry {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-weight: 700;
  }
</style>
